---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
---
<BaseLayout title="About" description="Editorial principles and operating model for streisandeffect.org.">
  <section class="prose">
    <p class="eyebrow">About the archive</p>
    <h1>Preservation for scrutiny, not sanitization.</h1>
    <p class="lede">
      streisandeffect.org exists to preserve records that are at risk of disappearing from public view. The goal is to make controversial or inconvenient documents easier to examine with context, provenance, and verification data.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="section grid">
    <article class="card">
      <h3>Public-interest threshold</h3>
      <p>We prioritize documents from institutions, public campaigns, advocacy organizations, governments, and other actors whose published materials affect public debate or policy.</p>
    </article>
    <article class="card">
      <h3>Factual provenance</h3>
      <p>Collection pages should state what is known: who published the material, where it appeared, when it disappeared or changed, and what evidence supports that history.</p>
    </article>
    <article class="card">
      <h3>Editorial separation</h3>
      <p>Pages should clearly separate original-document description, archive rationale, criticism, and opinion. When claims are contested, say so plainly.</p>
    </article>
  </section>

  <section class="section prose">
    <h2>Hosting does not imply endorsement of hostile material</h2>
    <p>
      The archive is pro-trans. It is not neutral about anti-trans politics or censorship pressure from gender-critical campaigns. When documents are preserved because they condemn anti-rights organizing, the archive should say that clearly.
    </p>
    <p>
      Some future preserved documents may include harmful framing, bigotry, misinformation, or contested claims. Hosting those materials, when it happens, preserves a record for scrutiny, research, and accountability rather than endorsing the hostile claims inside them.
    </p>
    <p>
      Collection pages should include content warnings and relevant rebuttals or subject-matter context when available.
    </p>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
